WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:10.840
The iShell is an alternative to using shell commands. For a ZOS user familiar with iSPF,

2
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:16.040
this can be easier to use. If you've completed any of the lab exercises so far, you'll also

3
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.880
be familiar with the iShell. Most of the functions that can be performed using the

4
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.400
iShell are related to the file system. A regular user can use the iShell to work with

5
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:31.960
directories, regular files, FIFO special files and symbolic links. A sysadmin can do all

6
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.960
of this and a good deal more, including mount and unmount a file system, set up ZOS Unix

7
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:44.200
users and groups, change attributes for ZOS Unix users, create character special files and

8
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.880
set up the root file system. Now if you look across the top here, you'll see a number

9
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:55.880
of pulldown menus and we're showing the directory menu here. The first option, directory

10
00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.840
list, has been enhanced to include all sorts of features such as sorting columns, using

11
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:07.080
color highlighting and executing actions based upon cursor location. Here is the directory

12
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:15.080
list and it, you ready for this? Lists the files of a directory. Other new features of

13
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.960
the directory list since ZOS version 1.6 include wildcard support on the directory list and an

14
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.960
option to inherit the command line position from the previous dialogue. Sorting is available

15
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.440
for any column that can be displayed even if that column is not selected for display.

16
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:38.600
A secondary sort column can also be specified. This support allows you to arrange the list

17
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:44.680
by file type for example and within file type by another attribute such as name, date or size.

18
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.880
The highlighting enhancements allow entries in the directory list to be highlighted based on

19
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.000
a number of different criteria. There are no defaults defined so if you want to highlight

20
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.320
entries in the directory list, you must set it up to do so. The terminal must support colors for

21
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.600
this feature to work. The use of this feature on terminals that don't support color might

22
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:13.640
produce some interesting and wildly arbitrary highlighting for file names. We can set the

23
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.200
highlighting criteria for things like when the sticky bit is on, when a file is marked as

24
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:27.160
executable and so forth. Support has been added to ISPF for the processing of ZOS Unix files.

25
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:36.040
This support includes the ability to edit, browse, create, delete, rename, copy and replace

26
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:43.400
ZOS Unix files. This new support is implemented as a directory list utility known as the ZOS

27
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:50.280
Unix directory list utility available as option 17 on the ISPF utilities menu. This initial

28
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.760
implementation of the directory list utility provides a subset of the functions that are

29
00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.800
supported by iShell. The set of functions supported by the directory list facility are

30
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:05.320
aimed to assist with basic tasks undertaken by programmers. The utility is not really aimed at

31
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:12.360
users like the ZOS Unix file system admins. Now as much as possible, the directory list

32
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.720
utility is designed to behave and support commands similar to the data set list utility

33
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:24.040
which is ISPF option 3.4 and that's done on purpose to assist users who are familiar with

34
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.400
the operation of the data set list utility but maybe have less experience working with ZOS Unix

35
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:37.880
files through utilities such as OMVS or iShell. When option 17 on the ISPF utility selection panel

36
00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:45.240
so that's ISPF option 3.17 is entered the ZOS Unix directory list utility entry panel is displayed.

37
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:51.160
This panel supports the following options blank to display a list of files in a directory and

38
00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:57.400
p to print a list of files in a directory to the ISPF list data set. For either option the user

39
00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.360
must enter in the path name field the path name of the directory they want to display or to

40
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:10.040
print. If a path name is not entered well then the user's home path is assumed. The ZOS Unix directory

41
00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.080
list panel is displayed when the option field is left blank and enter is pressed on the entry panel.

42
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:21.080
This panel lists all the files in a directory. The information for each entry in the directory

43
00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:26.440
is displayed in column fields across the screen. Now keep in mind that the number of columns

44
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:32.840
displayed depends upon the available screen width. This example here shows the initial directory

45
00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:39.240
list display on a terminal with a screen width of 85 and a screen depth of 31.

46
00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:48.200
Horizontal scrolling is supported using the right and left primary commands or function keys 11 and

47
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:54.920
10. This example shows the directory list display after entering the command right 5 on

48
00:04:54.920 --> 00:05:00.680
the directory list shown on a previous screenshot. The order in which columns appear in the directory

49
00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.720
list display and the size of the fields can be customized using the ZOS Unix directory list

50
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.160
column arrangement panel. This panel is accessed by using the pull down menu displayed under

51
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.720
options on the action bar on the ZOS Unix directory list entry and display panels.

52
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.360
Here the user can change the order in which the columns are displayed and the width of

53
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.960
the column fields. If a column fields width is set to zero the column is not displayed

54
00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:32.360
in the directory list. In the next video we'll discuss how to perform tasks by using the

55
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.920
directory list utility panels.
